<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="sec-maxima-minima-problems">
	<title>Optimization problems and critical points</title>

    <p xml:id="sec-u3-critical-points-gradient-descent-2-1">
        Directional derivatives describe first-order change along a chosen direction. We now use them to identify candidates for local extrema and to motivate an iterative method for decreasing a function.
      </p>

		<p xml:id="sec-maxima-minima-problems-2-2">
			In this unit, we apply multivariate calculus to solve <em>optimization problems</em>.
		</p>

		<definition xml:id="def-8-1-optimization">
			<title>Optimization problems</title>

			<p>
				Let <m>f: \R^n \to \R</m> be a scalar-valued function, and consider a set <m>U \subseteq \R^n</m>. Then we can consider two optimization problems, in which we call <m>f</m> the <term>objective function</term> of the problem, and <m>U</m> the <term>feasible region</term> of the optimization problem:
				<ul>
					<li>
						The <term>minimization problem</term> of <m>f</m> on <m>U</m> is the problem of finding <m>\mathbf{x}^* \in U</m> such that the value <m>f(\mathbf{x}^*)</m> is <em>smallest</em> among all elements of <m>U</m> (so the function is <em>minimized</em> on <m>U</m> by <m>\mathbf{x}^*</m>).
					</li>

					<li>
						The <term>maximization problem</term> of <m>f</m> on <m>U</m> is the problem of finding <m>\mathbf{x}^* \in U</m> such that the value <m>f(\mathbf{x}^*)</m> is <em>largest</em> among all elements of <m>U</m> (so the function is <em>maximized</em> on <m>U</m> by <m>\mathbf{x}^*</m>).
					</li>
				</ul>
			</p>
		</definition>

		<definition xml:id="sec-maxima-minima-problems-3-2">
			<title>Minima and maxima</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Suppose <m>f</m> is a scalar-valued function. A point <m>\mathbf{a}</m> in the domain of <m>f</m> is a

					<ul>
					<li><term>global minimum</term> if <m>f(\mathbf{a}) \leq f(\mathbf{x})</m> for all <m>\mathbf{x}</m> in the domain of <m>f</m>. In this case, <m>f(\mathbf{a})</m> is the <term>global minimum value</term>, or <term>absolute minimum value</term>, of the function <m>f</m>.</li>

					<li><term>global maximum</term> if <m>f(\mathbf{a}) \geq f(\mathbf{x})</m> for all <m>\mathbf{x}</m> in the domain of <m>f</m>. In this case, <m>f(\mathbf{a})</m> is the <term>global maximum value</term>, or <term>absolute maximum value</term>, of the function <m>f</m>.</li>

						<li><term>local minimum</term> if there is a ball centered at <m>\mathbf{a}</m> such that <m>f(\mathbf{a}) \leq f(\mathbf{x})</m> whenever <m>\mathbf{x}</m> is in that ball and also in the domain of <m>f</m>; in other words, whenever <m>\mathbf{x}</m> is close enough to <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and lies in the domain of <m>f</m>. In this case, we say <m>f(\mathbf{a})</m> is a <term>local minimum value</term> for the function <m>f</m>.</li>

						<li><term>local maximum</term> if there is a ball centered at <m>\mathbf{a}</m> such that <m>f(\mathbf{a}) \geq f(\mathbf{x})</m> whenever <m>\mathbf{x}</m> is in that ball and also in the domain of <m>f</m>; in other words, whenever <m>\mathbf{x}</m> is close enough to <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and lies in the domain of <m>f</m>. In this case, we say <m>f(\mathbf{a})</m> is a <term>local maximum value</term> for the function <m>f</m>.</li>
					</ul>

					A local minimum or local maximum is also called a <term>local extremum</term>.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>

		<remark xml:id="sec-maxima-minima-problems-3-3">
			<statement>
				<p>
					The graph of the function <m>-f</m> is obtained by reflecting the graph of the function <m>f</m> in the output axis. This operation turns local and global maxima into local and global minima, and vice versa. So methods for finding minima for a general function often give methods for finding maxima, which makes the two problems often equivalent.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</remark>

		<definition xml:id="def-u3-critical-point">
			<title>Critical points</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Suppose <m>f</m> is a scalar-valued function that is differentiable at a point <m>\mathbf{a}</m>. Then we say <m>\mathbf{a}</m> is a <term>critical point</term> if <m>\nabla f(\mathbf{a}) = \mathbf{0}</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>

		<p xml:id="sec-maxima-minima-problems-3-5">
			For a scalar-valued function of two variables, for example, <m>f(x,y)</m>, a critical point is a solution to the two equations <m>f_x(x,y) = 0</m> and <m>f_y(x,y) = 0</m>.
		</p>

		<p xml:id="sec-maxima-minima-problems-3-6">
			Just as the derivative of a scalar-valued function of a single variable helps identify local minima and maxima, partial derivatives provide information about possible local minima/maxima.
		</p>

		<theorem xml:id="sec-maxima-minima-problems-3-7">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Suppose <m>f</m> is differentiable at an <xref ref="sec-8-4-interior-and-exterior" text="custom">interior point</xref> <m>\mathbf a</m> of its domain. If <m>\mathbf a</m> is a local extremum of <m>f</m>, then
					<me>\nabla f(\mathbf a)=\mathbf 0.</me>
				</p>
			</statement>

			<proof>
				<p>
					Let <m>\mathbf v</m> be a unit vector, and define
					<me>g(t)=f(\mathbf a+t\mathbf v).</me>
					Because <m>\mathbf a</m> is an interior local extremum of <m>f</m>, the function <m>g</m> has a local extremum at <m>t=0</m>. Therefore
					<me>g'(0)=0.</me>
					By <xref ref="thm-10-1-direction-in-terms-of-gradient"/>,
					<me>g'(0)=D_{\mathbf v}f(\mathbf a)=\nabla f(\mathbf a)\cdot\mathbf v.</me>
					This holds for every unit vector <m>\mathbf v</m>. If <m>\nabla f(\mathbf a)\neq\mathbf 0</m>, choose
					<me>\mathbf v=\frac{\nabla f(\mathbf a)}{\|\nabla f(\mathbf a)\|}.</me>
					Then
					<me>\nabla f(\mathbf a)\cdot\mathbf v=\|\nabla f(\mathbf a)\|>0,</me>
					a contradiction. Hence <m>\nabla f(\mathbf a)=\mathbf0</m>.
				</p>
			</proof>
		</theorem>

		<warning xml:id="warning-u3-critical-point-necessary-not-sufficient">
			<title>A necessary condition is not sufficient</title>
			<p>
				For a function differentiable at an interior point <m>\mathbf a</m>, <xref ref="sec-maxima-minima-problems-3-7"/> says that <m>\nabla f(\mathbf a)=\mathbf0</m> is a <em>necessary</em> condition for a local extremum, but it is <em>not sufficient</em>. A critical point need not be a local minimum or a local maximum.
			</p>
		</warning>

		<example xml:id="act-u3-critical-point-saddle-quadratic">
      <title>Critical points of a difference of squares (U3-LO9)</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the critical points of <m>f(x,y) = x^2 - y^2</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>

			<solution>
				<p>
					We compute that <m>f_x(x,y) = 2x</m> and <m>f_y(x,y) = -2y</m>. The only <m>(x,y)</m> such that <m>f_x(x,y) = 2x = 0</m> and <m>f_y(x,y) = -2y = 0</m> is <m>(0,0)</m>. This is the only critical point.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>

		<activity xml:id="act-u3-critical-point-quadratic-minimum">
      <title>Critical points of a quadratic function (U3-LO9)</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the critical points of the function
					<me>f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2 - 2x + 4y</me>.
				</p>
			</statement>

			<solution>
				<p>
					To find the critical points, we calculate the partial derivatives and set them equal to zero. We find that
					<me>
						f_x(x,y) = 2x - 2 \quad\text{and}\quad f_y(x,y) = 2y + 4
					</me>.
					The critical points are the pairs <m>(x,y)</m> that simultaneously solve the equations <m>2x - 2 = 0</m> and <m>2y + 4 = 0</m>. So the only critical point is <m>(1, -2)</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>

    <activity xml:id="act-u3-critical-points-row-reduction">
      <title>Critical points via row reduction (U3-LO9, U2-LO2)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find all critical points of
          <me>f(x,y,z)=x^2+xy+yz+z^2-x-2y-z.</me>
          Write the critical-point equations as an augmented matrix, use Gaussian elimination to obtain row echelon form, and finish by back-substitution.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The polynomial <m>f</m> is differentiable everywhere, and its gradient is
          <me>
            \nabla f(x,y,z)=
            \begin{bmatrix}
              2x+y-1\\
              x+z-2\\
              y+2z-1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          Thus its critical points are exactly the solutions of
          <me>
            \begin{cases}
              2x+y=1,\\
              x+z=2,\\
              y+2z=1.
            \end{cases}
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Following <xref ref="alg-gaussian-elimination"/>, we first swap rows to obtain a leading pivot of <m>1</m>. We then eliminate below each pivot and normalize the final pivot:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
                2\amp1\amp0\amp1\\
                1\amp0\amp1\amp2\\
                0\amp1\amp2\amp1
              \end{array}\right]
              \amp\xrightarrow{R_1\leftrightarrow R_2}
              \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1\amp0\amp1\amp2\\
                2\amp1\amp0\amp1\\
                0\amp1\amp2\amp1
              \end{array}\right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp\xrightarrow{R_2\to R_2-2R_1}
              \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1\amp0\amp1\amp2\\
                0\amp1\amp-2\amp-3\\
                0\amp1\amp2\amp1
              \end{array}\right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp\xrightarrow{R_3\to R_3-R_2}
              \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1\amp0\amp1\amp2\\
                0\amp1\amp-2\amp-3\\
                0\amp0\amp4\amp4
              \end{array}\right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp\xrightarrow{R_3\to\frac14R_3}
              \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1\amp0\amp1\amp2\\
                0\amp1\amp-2\amp-3\\
                0\amp0\amp1\amp1
              \end{array}\right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The last row gives <m>z=1</m>. Back-substitution into the second row, <m>y-2z=-3</m>, and then the first row, <m>x+z=2</m>, gives
          <md>
            <mrow>y\amp=-3+2z=-3+2(1)=-1,</mrow>
            <mrow>x\amp=2-z=2-1=1.</mrow>
          </md>
          There is a pivot in every variable column and no inconsistent row, so the system has a unique solution. Therefore the only critical point of <m>f</m> is <m>(1,-1,1)</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <p>
      The next critical-point system leads to a quadratic equation. Recall that, when <m>a\ne0</m>, the real solutions of <m>at^2+bt+c=0</m> are given by the <em>quadratic formula</em>
      <me>t=\frac{-b\pm\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a},</me>
      provided <m>b^2-4ac\ge0</m>. If <m>b^2-4ac&lt;0</m>, there are no real solutions.
    </p>

    <activity xml:id="act-u3-critical-points-quadratic-formula">
      <title>Critical points using the quadratic formula (U3-LO9)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find all critical points of
          <me>f(x,y)=x^3+2xy+y^2-2x.</me>
          Use the quadratic formula to solve the resulting quadratic equation.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The partial derivatives are
          <me>f_x(x,y)=3x^2+2y-2,\qquad f_y(x,y)=2x+2y.</me>
          Setting them equal to zero gives
          <me>
            \begin{cases}
              3x^2+2y-2=0,\\
              2x+2y=0.
            \end{cases}
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The second equation gives <m>y=-x</m>. Substituting into the first equation yields
          <me>3x^2-2x-2=0.</me>
          With <m>a=3</m>, <m>b=-2</m>, and <m>c=-2</m>, the quadratic formula gives
          <me>x=\frac{2\pm\sqrt{(-2)^2-4(3)(-2)}}{2(3)}=\frac{2\pm\sqrt{28}}{6}=\frac{1\pm\sqrt7}{3}.</me>
        </p>
        <p>
          For each root, use <m>y=-x</m>. Thus the two critical points are
          <me>
            \left(\frac{1+\sqrt7}{3},\frac{-1-\sqrt7}{3}\right)
            \quad\text{and}\quad
            \left(\frac{1-\sqrt7}{3},\frac{-1+\sqrt7}{3}\right).
          </me>
          These are all the critical points because every solution of the system must satisfy <m>y=-x</m> and one of the two quadratic roots above.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <p>
      When a critical-point equation factors as <m>(x-a)(y-b)=0</m>, at least one factor must be zero: <m>x=a</m> or <m>y=b</m>, possibly both. Treat these as two cases, substitute each into the remaining equation, and combine the solutions, listing any repeated point only once. Dividing by either factor without considering when it is zero can discard valid solutions.
    </p>

    <activity xml:id="act-u3-critical-points-zero-product">
      <title>Critical points by splitting into cases (U3-LO9)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find all critical points of
          <me>f(x,y)=(x-1)^2(y-2)+y^2-8y.</me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The partial derivatives are
          <md>
            <mrow>f_x(x,y)\amp=2(x-1)(y-2),</mrow>
            <mrow>f_y(x,y)\amp=(x-1)^2+2y-8.</mrow>
          </md>
          Setting them equal to zero and dividing the first equation by <m>2</m> gives
          <me>
            \begin{cases}
              (x-1)(y-2)=0,\\
              (x-1)^2+2y-8=0.
            \end{cases}
          </me>
          The first equation gives two cases, and in each case the second equation must also hold.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              If <m>x=1</m>, the second equation becomes <m>2y-8=0</m>, so <m>y=4</m>. This gives the critical point <m>(1,4)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If <m>y=2</m>, the second equation becomes
              <me>(x-1)^2-4=0.</me>
              Thus <m>x-1=\pm2</m>, giving <m>x=-1</m> or <m>x=3</m>. This gives the critical points <m>(-1,2)</m> and <m>(3,2)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Therefore all critical points are
          <me>(1,4),\qquad (-1,2),\qquad (3,2).</me>
          Each point satisfies both equations, and the two cases exhaust the solutions of the first equation.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

		<warning xml:id="warning-critical-points-candidates">
			<title>Critical points are candidates</title>
			<p>
				The equation <m>\nabla f=0</m> finds candidates for local extrema. It does not by itself find boundary extrema, constrained extrema, or absolute extrema. Always combine critical-point information with the domain and boundary conditions.
			</p>
		</warning>

    <activity xml:id="act-u3-prediction-loss-flat-direction">
      <title>Redundant features give a flat direction of the loss (U3-LO6, U2-LO6)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Recall <xref ref="u2-anatomy-linear-map" text="type-global"/>'s redundant-feature example in <xref ref="u2-act-preview-redundant-features"/>. Add a third feature to our data by summing the original two feature columns:
          <me>
            \widetilde X=\begin{bmatrix}1\amp0\amp1\\0\amp1\amp1\\1\amp1\amp2\end{bmatrix},
            \qquad \mathbf{y}=\begin{bmatrix}1\\2\\2\end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          There are now three weights <m>\boldsymbol\theta=(\theta_1,\theta_2,\theta_3)</m>, with loss
          <me>\widetilde{\mathcal L}(\boldsymbol\theta)=\|\widetilde X\boldsymbol\theta-\mathbf{y}\|^2.</me>
          The predictions depend only on the two combinations <m>w_1=\theta_1+\theta_3</m> and <m>w_2=\theta_2+\theta_3</m>, so <m>\widetilde{\mathcal L}(\boldsymbol\theta)=\mathcal L(\theta_1+\theta_3,\theta_2+\theta_3)</m>.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Verify that <m>\mathbf{z}=(-1,-1,1)</m> is in the null space of <m>\widetilde X</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Show that <m>\widetilde{\mathcal L}(\boldsymbol\theta+t\mathbf{z})=\widetilde{\mathcal L}(\boldsymbol\theta)</m> for every real <m>t</m>. Evaluate this constant loss along the line through <m>\boldsymbol\theta_0=(1,1,0)</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>What is <m>\frac{d}{dt}\widetilde{\mathcal L}(\boldsymbol\theta+t\mathbf{z})</m>? If a minimizing weight vector is found, can it be the unique minimizing weight vector?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Direct multiplication verifies the given direction:
              <me>
                \widetilde X\mathbf{z}
                =\begin{bmatrix}-1+1\\-1+1\\-1-1+2\end{bmatrix}
                =\mathbf{0}.
              </me>
              Decreasing each original weight by <m>t</m> and increasing the redundant-feature weight by <m>t</m> leaves both effective weights unchanged.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Unit 2's linearity identity gives
              <me>
                \widetilde X(\boldsymbol\theta+t\mathbf{z})-\mathbf{y}
                =\widetilde X\boldsymbol\theta-\mathbf{y}
                  +t\widetilde X\mathbf{z}
                =\widetilde X\boldsymbol\theta-\mathbf{y}.
              </me>
              Taking squared lengths proves equality of the losses for every <m>t</m>. At <m>\boldsymbol\theta_0</m>, the predictions are <m>(1,1,2)</m> and the loss is <m>1</m>. Every point <m>(1-t,1-t,t)</m> gives these same predictions and the same loss.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The loss along the line is constant, so its derivative is zero for every <m>t</m>. By the chain rule, <m>\nabla\widetilde{\mathcal L}(\boldsymbol\theta)^T\mathbf{z}=0</m>. If <m>\boldsymbol\theta_*</m> minimizes the loss, all distinct vectors <m>\boldsymbol\theta_*+t\mathbf{z}</m> minimize it as well. The data therefore cannot identify these three weights uniquely.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <note xml:id="note-u3-exact-invariance-first-order-change">
      <title>Exact invariance and first-order change</title>
      <p>
        For a linear prediction map <m>\boldsymbol\theta\mapsto\widetilde X\boldsymbol\theta</m>, any direction <m>\mathbf z</m> satisfying <m>\widetilde X\mathbf z=\mathbf 0</m> leaves the predictions, and therefore the loss, unchanged along the entire line <m>\boldsymbol\theta+t\mathbf z</m>. This is the exact invariance illustrated in <xref ref="act-u3-prediction-loss-flat-direction"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a differentiable nonlinear map <m>F</m>, the condition <m>J_F(\mathbf a)\mathbf z=\mathbf 0</m> guarantees only zero first-order change at <m>\mathbf a</m>. The output <m>F(\mathbf a+t\mathbf z)</m> may still vary with <m>t</m>, as in <xref ref="act-u3-sympy-local-nullspace"/>. Also, equal loss values do not necessarily mean equal predictions: different prediction vectors can have the same squared error, as <xref ref="act-u3-prediction-loss-model"/> illustrates.
      </p>
    </note>

</subsection>
